@startuml
title Rate Limiter Class Diagram

enum CallerType {
    PAID
    FREE
}

class LimitRule {
    -maxRequests: int
    -windowSeconds: int
}

class CallerState {
    -callerId: String
    -timestamps: Deque<Instant>
    -lock: ReentrantLock
}

class Response {
    -allowed: boolean
    -message: String
    -retryAfterSeconds: Long
    +{static} allowed(): Response
    +{static} rejected(message: String, retryAfterSeconds: long): Response
}

class RateLimiter {
    -callers: ConcurrentHashMap<String, CallerState>
    -rules: ConcurrentHashMap<CallerType, LimitRule>
    -defaultRule: LimitRule
    -clock: Clock
    +process(callerId: String, callerType: CallerType): Response
    +updateRule(callerType: CallerType, rule: LimitRule): void
    +removeIdleCallers(): void
}

class IdleCallerRemover {
    -limiter: RateLimiter
    -intervalSeconds: long
    -scheduler: ScheduledExecutorService
    +start(): void
    +stop(): void
}

RateLimiter "1" *-- "0..*" CallerState : callers
RateLimiter "1" --> "0..*" LimitRule : rules
RateLimiter "1" --> "1" LimitRule : defaultRule
RateLimiter ..> CallerType
RateLimiter ..> Response : creates
IdleCallerRemover --> "1" RateLimiter : calls removeIdleCallers()

note right of CallerState
    Lock reason: one request changes only
    its own caller's queue. Two different
    callers never share data, so one lock
    per caller is enough. In L15 every
    operation changed the shared linked
    list, so one lock covered everything.
    Rule: the lock covers exactly the data
    one operation changes.
end note

note bottom of RateLimiter
    Clock is passed in the constructor so a
    test can fix the time (1:04:59, 1:05:01).
    Rule not found for a type: use defaultRule.
end note

note bottom of IdleCallerRemover
    The remover owns the schedule and calls
    limiter.removeIdleCallers(). The limiter
    owns the cleaning logic and does not know
    about the remover.
end note
@enduml